HST Office: a toolbar control ignores a state that arrives after it and its toolbox were disposed.

Cycling the user interface modes with two documents of one module open crashed the office (SIGSEGV
in ToolBox::ImplFormat <- ToolBox::GetItemRect <- SvxClipBoardControl::StateChangedAtToolBoxControl
<- SfxToolBoxControl::statusChanged <- SfxDispatchController_Impl::addStatusListener <-
svt::ToolboxController::bindListener <- framework::ToolBarManager::UpdateControllers, from the
toolbar's 50 ms update timer). Upstream code, same in 26.8; seen with HST 1.0.1 and every 1.0.2 build.

Traced on Linux (gdb on the release build with its symbol table, mode switches over UNO):
1. a mode switch creates the standard toolbar with its paste control and starts the toolbar's
   delayed controller update;
2. the main thread runs that update: binding the paste control asks for its first state, the clipboard
   formats (SwBaseShell::StateClpbrd -> TransferableDataHelper::CreateFromSystemClipboard ->
   gtk_clipboard_wait_for_targets), which runs a nested main loop with the SolarMutex released;
3. the next mode switch on the UNO request thread takes the SolarMutex and destroys that toolbar
   (SfxViewFrame::ShowChildWindow -> SfxDispatcher::Update_Impl -> SfxWorkWindow::UpdateObjectBars_Impl2
   -> LayoutManager::destroyElement -> ToolBarManager::dispose): the controls are disposed, then the
   ToolBox, whose data is released;
4. the main thread gets the SolarMutex back and addStatusListener hands the state to the disposed paste
   control, which lays out the disposed ToolBox: read of its released data.
Not observed, but the same can happen without a second thread: the nested main loop also runs pending events.

Two fixes, both needed (with the first alone the same run crashed one step later, in step 5):
- SfxToolBoxControl::statusChanged returns when the control is disposed or its toolbox is gone or
  disposed (checked under the SolarMutex, which the dispose holds). Every SfxToolBoxControl state goes
  through statusChanged, so no control of this kind touches a disposed toolbox.
- 5. ToolBarManager::UpdateControllers then went on iterating m_aControllerMap, which RemoveControllers()
  had cleared meanwhile: read of a freed hash node (UpdateControllers+0x347, `mov 0x10(%rbx),%r15`). It
  now walks a copy of the controllers and updates one only while it is still the toolbar's controller
  for its item (a refilled toolbar updates its new controllers itself; hst-toolbar-no-update-while-
  removing-controllers.patch already keeps the timer out while the controllers are being removed).
Stress check: tests/runtime/check-ui-mode-cycle-stress.py.

